{"name":"Snow Ballet","hex":"#def1e7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The partly frozen, crystalline state of water that falls from the atmosphere as precipitation in flakes; also, the falling of such flakes; and the accumulation of them on the ground or on objects as a white layer.\n\n An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm.","An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Preceded by the dummy subject it: to have snow (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) fall from the atmosphere.","Of a thing: to fall like snow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow"},{"term":"ballet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A classical form of dance.","A theatrical presentation of such dancing, usually with music, sometimes in the form of a story."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To perform an action reminiscent of ballet dancing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow","wikidata":"Q7561"},{"title":"Ballet","blocks":[{"text":"Ballet is a type of performance dance that originated during the Italian Renaissance in the fifteenth century and later developed into a concert dance form in France and Russia. It has since become a widespread and highly technical form of dance with its own vocabulary. Ballet has been influential globally and has defined the foundational techniques which are used in many other dance genres and cultures. Various schools around the world have incorporated their own cultures. As a result, ballet has evolved in distinct ways."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Edgar_Degas_-_La_Classe_de_danse.jpg/330px-Edgar_Degas_-_La_Classe_de_danse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballet","wikidata":"Q41425"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Feb242007_blizzard.gif/500px-Feb242007_blizzard.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Feb242007_blizzard.gif/1280px-Feb242007_blizzard.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x",